= Pareto principle =

== Overview ==

{{:Pareto principle/Simple}}

The '''Pareto principle''' is a heuristic about concentration of outcomes among contributors, often rendered as the 80/20 rule. It should be distinguished from Pareto's empirical studies of income distributions, the mathematical Pareto family of heavy-tailed distributions and the unrelated normative concept of Pareto efficiency. None states that exactly 20% of causes produce exactly 80% of effects in every system.

== Provenance and claims ==

Wilfredo/Vilfredo Pareto's nineteenth-century empirical inquiry concerned unequal income distributions. Later quality engineering, especially Joseph Juran, generalized the “vital few” idea to defect categories and managerial attention. Juran's own retrospective warns about treating his later broad generalization as Pareto's original discovery.<ref name="juran_core">Joseph M. Juran, [https://www.juran.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/03/The-Non-Pareto-Principle-1974.pdf ''The Non-Pareto Principle'' (1974)], first-person historical clarification of the naming and the extension into general management.</ref><ref name="history_core">[https://doi.org/10.1007/s12232-024-00458-7 ''Pareto and probability distributions''], ''International Review of Economics'' (2024), archival reconstruction of Pareto's statistical work.</ref>

== Proper empirical use ==

A Pareto chart sorts quantified categories by their contribution to a chosen outcome and may report a cumulative share. Its legitimacy requires a stable observation period, mutually clear categories, a consistent denominator and a statement of whether categories are causes, symptoms or correlates. The same dataset can look different when categories are merged, subdivided or measured by monetary cost rather than frequency.

The relation to [[Related to::Causality|causality]] is important: concentration is descriptive until mechanisms and intervention effects have been identified. It also informs investigation prioritization and resource allocation only under declared costs and objectives; these related pages currently exist in separate unmerged PRs and are therefore not hard linked here.

== Statistical limits ==

For a continuous nonnegative quantity, the share possessed by the top fraction of a population depends on its distribution. Even under a textbook Pareto Type I model, a particular 80/20 split requires a specific tail parameter. A concentration chart alone cannot establish a power-law distribution or prove the presence of a causal “vital few.”

